1. The Extraction Phase (MediaPipe)
When a video is loaded, OpenCV reads it frame-by-frame. It hands each frame to MediaPipe, which maps out the human body in 3D space. 
It specifically tracks 75 points: 33 on the body/pose, 21 on the left hand, and 21 on the right hand.

Result: A raw 3D skeleton matrix saved as an .npy file.

2. The Kinematic Preprocessing Phase
Raw coordinates aren't enough for the ASL—motion, so our process_seq() function centers the skeleton (so it doesn't matter where the user stands in the frame), 
scales it (so it doesn't matter how tall they are), and calculates the Velocity and Acceleration of every joint. It then standardizes the clip to exactly 100 frames.
Result: A normalized, dynamic motion map.

3. The Fingerprinting Phase (Graph Encoder)
This is where the Keras 3 model comes in (which was pain in the ass to get working properly, prob was why it wasn't working for you earlier). The script loads only the Graph_Encoder, ie the isolated brain of our AI.

It feeds the motion map into the custom Spacial Graph Convolutions, Mamba blocks, and Transformers (which was actually really cool, I've never thought of stacking the neural network blocks like that, they feed into each other to better understand the data). The model digests all that complex motion and spits out a single, dense 1D array (an embedding). Which is how we find the unique id for the signs.

4. The Siamese Comparison Phase
Instead of using a heavy AI layer to compare the video, you use pure mathematics.

The script loads our reference_library.json, which contains the pre-computed fingerprints for all 2000 signs. It calculates the absolute distance between the user's fingerprint and every reference fingerprint, converts that distance into a confidence percentage (similarity score), 
and sorts them to find the perfect match.

